What: on macOS before 10.13 (Darwin 17), sun.nio.fs normalizes paths (to NFD on the way to the
  file system, back to NFC on the way to Java) by default, as JDK 19 and earlier did everywhere.
  An explicit -Djdk.nio.path.useNormalizationFormD=<true|false> still decides, as upstream.
Why on 10.9: there is no APFS before 10.13; HFS+ stores every name decomposed (NFD), so without
  normalization a file created under an NFC name lists back as a different, NFD string and
  Path.equals fails for any non-ASCII name (java/nio/file/Path/MacPathTest.java). Upstream
  turned the default off in JDK 20 because APFS preserves names as written.
Provenance: ours (ruling R27). The version test uses os.version (jdk.internal.util.OSVersion),
  which patches/0004 keeps working on 10.9; macOS 10.13 is Darwin 17.
Exit: none while 10.9 is supported; on 10.13 and later the default is upstream's.

--- a/src/java.base/macosx/classes/sun/nio/fs/MacOSXFileSystem.java
+++ b/src/java.base/macosx/classes/sun/nio/fs/MacOSXFileSystem.java
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 package sun.nio.fs;
 
 import java.util.regex.Pattern;
+import jdk.internal.util.OSVersion;
 
 import static sun.nio.fs.MacOSXNativeDispatcher.*;
 
@@ -43,8 +44,12 @@
     static {
         final String name = PROPERTY_NORMALIZE_FILE_PATHS;
         String value = System.getProperty(name);
+        // Mavergreen: before macOS 10.13 (Darwin 17) there is no APFS, and HFS+ stores names
+        // decomposed (NFD), so paths are normalized unless -Djdk.nio.path.useNormalizationFormD
+        // says otherwise.
         NORMALIZE_FILE_PATHS = (value != null)
-            && ("".equals(value) || Boolean.parseBoolean(value));
+            ? ("".equals(value) || Boolean.parseBoolean(value))
+            : OSVersion.current().compareTo(new OSVersion(10, 13)) < 0;
     }
 
